./bootstrap.sh

last stable build: 2026-07-21


## file structure
-----------------

```
dotfiles/
├── bootstrap.sh           # repair, build, and activate in one command
├── flake.nix              # entry point
├── bin/
│   └── nix-repair-boot    # recover the encrypted Nix APFS volume after boot
├── lib/mksystem.nix       # system builder helper
├── machines/
│   └── macbook/
│       └── default.nix    # system + home-manager + homebrew config
└── config/                # shared dotfiles
    ├── aerospace/
    ├── claude/
    ├── ghostty/
    ├── nvim/
    └── sh/
```

## what gets installed
----------------------

via nix (home-manager):
- cli: ripgrep, fzf, eza, tree, curl, jq, htop, ncdu
- dev: uv, gh, git-lfs, cmake, gnumake
- languages: python312, nodejs_22
- media: ffmpeg, imagemagick
- editor: neovim (with treesitter, lsps)
- terminal: tmux (with floax, fzf, thumbs plugins)

via homebrew (managed by nix-darwin):
- brews: none; nixpkgs or bust!
- casks: ghostty@tip, mactex, claude-code

configs symlinked:
- ~/.config/nvim/
- ~/.config/ghostty/
- ~/.config/tmux/
- ~/.config/claude/
- ~/.config/kanata/kanata.kbd
- ~/.aerospace.toml
- ~/.zshrc

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## bootstrap (fresh machine)

1. install nix

```bash
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf -L https://install.determinate.systems/nix | sh -s -- install
```

2. install homebrew

```bash
/bin/bash -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)"
```

3. clone dotfiles

```bash
git clone https://github.com/spikedoanz/dotfiles ~/.config/dotfiles
cd ~/.config/dotfiles
```

4. repair (if needed), build, and deploy

```bash
./bootstrap.sh
```

this single command:
- repairs a reboot-broken Nix APFS volume and daemon
- builds the flake as the current user (avoids root Git ownership failures)
- uses sudo only to switch and activate the built generation
- installs all nix packages
- installs all homebrew packages
- symlinks all config files
- sets up tmux plugins
- configures the system


## notes
-------

macOS Mission Control binds Ctrl+Alt+Arrow to "Move left/right a space" by default.
This conflicts with VsCoq (Coq IDE) keybindings. Disable with:

```bash
defaults write com.apple.symbolichotkeys AppleSymbolicHotKeys -dict-add 80 '{ enabled = 0; value = { parameters = (65535, 123, 8781824); type = standard; }; }'
defaults write com.apple.symbolichotkeys AppleSymbolicHotKeys -dict-add 82 '{ enabled = 0; value = { parameters = (65535, 124, 8781824); type = standard; }; }'
```

Requires logout/login to take effect.

Nix boot repair:

macOS updates/reboots can leave the Nix APFS volume unmounted because the
`org.nixos.*` launch daemons are no longer loaded. The shell runs
`nix-repair-boot --auto` once per boot when `/nix` or the daemon socket is
missing. Run it manually if needed:

```bash
nix-repair-boot
```

You normally do not need to call this directly: `./bootstrap.sh` runs it before
building whenever Nix is unavailable.

If it still fails, enable the `sh` items in System Settings -> General -> Login
Items & Extensions -> Allow in the Background.

Sol image cache:

Sol writes copied images into `~/.config/sol/images_pasteboard`, which can grow
large and make the launcher feel slow. Keep that directory read-only/immutable:

```bash
sol-no-cache lock
sol-no-cache status
```

Undo if Sol changes behavior or image clipboard support is needed:

```bash
sol-no-cache unlock
```

Other Sol settings worth keeping quiet:
- `~/.config/sol/config.json` should keep `"searchFolders": []`.
- Sol Settings -> Save clipboard history should stay off unless needed.
- Sol Settings -> Show Browser Bookmarks can be disabled if bookmark search gets heavy.

Kanata keyboard remapping:

`nix-darwin` installs Kanata, validates/copies `kanata.kbd` into the Nix store,
and runs it at login as `org.spike.kanata`. The launch agent uses a narrowly
scoped sudo rule because macOS requires root access to intercept keyboards.
The nixpkgs build disables Kanata's security-sensitive `cmd` action.

macOS still requires two one-time, machine-local steps that cannot be made
hermetic: install and activate the signed Karabiner DriverKit VirtualHIDDevice,
then approve Kanata under System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Input
Monitoring. Kanata's release notes specify VirtualHIDDevice 6.2.0 for macOS
11 and newer:

https://github.com/jtroo/kanata/releases

Karabiner-Elements may supply the driver, but its remapper must not run at the
same time as Kanata. The `karabiner_grabber` process indicates a conflict; the
VirtualHIDDevice daemon itself is required.

After `./bootstrap.sh`, add `/run/current-system/sw/bin/kanata` in Input
Monitoring (press Command-Shift-G in the file picker to enter the path), then
restart the agent:

```bash
launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/org.spike.kanata
```

macOS may require Input Monitoring approval again after a Nix update changes
the Kanata executable's code signature.

Useful checks:

```bash
kanata --check --cfg ~/.config/kanata/kanata.kbd
launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/org.spike.kanata
tail -n 100 /tmp/kanata.err.log /tmp/kanata.out.log
pgrep -lf 'karabiner_grabber|Karabiner-VirtualHIDDevice-Daemon|kanata'
```


## adding a new machine

1. create `machines/<name>/default.nix`
2. add entry to `flake.nix`
3. deploy: `./bootstrap.sh <name>`
